<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Sicán</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 1375 CE (approx.) · capital Batán Grande</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A theocratic culture of Peru's north coast, centred on the pyramids of Batán Grande, that dominated Andean goldworking and trade in arsenical bronze.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sicán culture, also called Lambayeque, emerged around 750 in the La Leche and Lambayeque valleys, in the former territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/moche">Moche</a>. A legend recorded by the Spanish tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naymlap">Naymlap</a>, who arrived by sea with a fleet of balsa rafts and founded a dynasty there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Middle Sicán, about 900 to 1100, was ruled from the Sicán Precinct at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batan-grande">Batán Grande</a>, with large adobe pyramids such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-loro">Huaca Loro</a>. Elite tombs contained tonnes of grave goods, including gold masks and arsenical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. Sicán produced arsenical bronze on a large scale and used naipes, small copper axe-shaped objects, possibly as a form of currency. Trade reached <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a> for spondylus shell. Its art centred on the Sicán Lord, a figure with winged eyes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1100 a severe drought followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> floods led to the burning and abandonment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batan-grande">Batán Grande</a>. Late Sicán continued at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tucume">Túcume</a> until the region was conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor</a> around 1375.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sican.jpg" alt="Sicán" width="320" height="438"><figcaption>Image: Sican123 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grantocadolambayeque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 1375 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Batán Grande</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Naymlap</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sicán religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muchik</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lambayeque culture, Sican</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1062&amp;focus=sican" aria-label="Sicán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2470 2419 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sicán lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2790 2675L2814 2737"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2814 2737h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2790 2675h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2790 2675h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Batán Grande. <a href="/atlas?year=1062&amp;focus=sican">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
